WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.249
The libertarian movement has lost

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.559
its way over the past 60 years

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.479
as it shifted from Friedrich Hayek's

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.159
classical liberal corrective to

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.379
depression era central planning

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:11.549
to Murray Rothbard

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:12.550
to full-blown anarcho-capitalism

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.139
in which all taxation is theft

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.879
and all transfer payments are

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.169
immoral.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:21.239
That's the argument in a provocative

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.069
new book called Burning Down

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.959
the House How Libertarian

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.209
Philosophy Was Corrupted by Delusion

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.489
and Greed by Andrew Koppelman.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.319
Reason spoke with Koppelman, a law

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.579
professor at Northwestern

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.769
University, about why he believes

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.599
classical liberals have given ground

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.279
to anarchism and how that

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.109
fundamentally changes not just

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.879
the rhetoric, but the political

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.979
goals of the libertarian movement.

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:51.859
Andrew Koppelman, thanks for talking

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:52.339
to Reason.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:53.600
Happy to be here.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:56.749
All right. So here,

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:58.039
give me this.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:59.989
You're you're a law professor at

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.639
Northwestern University, right?

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.049
You've taught various places and

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:04.050
things like that.

00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:05.749
Let's start. What's.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:07.909
What's the elevator pitch of Burning

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:09.139
Down the House?

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:11.329
The elevator pitch is that

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.489
American libertarianism has

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:15.169
a valid core.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.929
The valid core is understanding

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:20.509
the value of free markets,

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.459
which is something that is not well

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.319
understood by everyone on

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.459
the left.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.439
But libertarianism

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.419
has increasingly taken

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.339
the form of an extreme

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:33.259
anti statism,

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:34.260
which

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.939
its proponents believe

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:40.219
will actually advance human liberty.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.769
If we cripple the capacities

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:44.749
of the American state, it's

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.049
not going to do that.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:47.899
And a large part of what

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.879
the state does is it

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.489
protects us from predators

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:54.439
and from forces that

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.389
aren't anybody's fault, but that

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:58.519
you need the state to control,

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.409
such as COVID is a good

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:01.819
example.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:03.799
And so

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.779
the interest in

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.689
crippling state capacity

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:10.519
has ended up being a coalition

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:12.859
of two different

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:13.729
groups of folks.

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:16.039
There are the folks who believe in

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:17.569
bad political philosophy.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.159
That's really the audience for my

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.019
book. It's people who are attracted

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:22.939
to this philosophy and who I want

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.739
to persuade to.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:26.478
To Bad political philosophy.

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:28.159
Bad Political philosophy, political

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.139
philosophy that is poorly

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:32.029
reasoned and

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:33.859
that will not lead to the

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.419
attractive results that it

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:37.399
advertises, will not lead

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.679
to an advance in human freedom

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.329
and politics.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:43.339
The desire to

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:45.439
cripple state capacity attracts

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.869
a coalition of idealists

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:49.729
who believe in that stuff

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:51.559
and unscrupulous

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.149
businessmen. Most businessmen are

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:54.709
very scrupulous, but the

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.539
unscrupulous ones are drawn

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:58.429
to this because it enables them to

00:02:58.430 --> 00:02:59.899
hurt people without being bothered

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:01.639
by the police and so.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:03.829
Well, delusion and greed.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:04.849
That's the delusion.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:06.109
And that's the greed.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:07.369
Okay.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:09.259
And to kind of put

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:11.089
some names on this,

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.069
essentially the book is

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.529
about how libertarianism

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:16.519
used to be.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.589
And I think your your,

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:20.779
you know, case here is pretty valid

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:22.879
in the mid-twentieth century or

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.169
in the second half of the 20th

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.179
century, libertarianism was

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:28.039
kind of dominated by the thought

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.199
of Friedrich Hayek and

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:33.109
Hayekian libertarianism was

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:34.969
clearly part of

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:37.189
a liberal political

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:39.469
philosophy, was on a range,

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:40.489
you know, and there were people to

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.469
the left and to the right.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:44.329
But he believed in things like

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:46.519
a functioning government,

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.009
social welfare spending,

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:50.839
you know,

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:52.249
I mean, basically, he believed in

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:53.839
limited government, more limited

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:55.249
than people on the left, less

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:56.659
limited or, you know, and more

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:57.919
limited than people on the right in

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:59.809
different ways. And that that

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:01.849
over time gave way ultimately

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:03.709
to a libertarianism, which is

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:05.269
really characterized by the thought

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:06.529
of Murray Rothbard.

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:07.639
Well, yeah.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.499
And as a species of

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:11.329
anarchism, I'd say it's a kind of

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:13.099
right wing anarcho capitalism.

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:14.269
Yep.

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:15.529
Okay.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:17.838
Let's start with the the controlling

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:19.969
metaphor of the book

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:22.009
Burning Down the House, because you

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:23.929
start out and you come back to

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.579
a story from a few years ago.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:27.139
What what is what are you

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:28.939
referencing in the title?

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:31.609
The story is an episode

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.159
in Obion County, Tennessee.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.809
The county doesn't have its own fire

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:37.999
department, and so it contracted

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.799
with a nearby fire department.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:41.599
Or more precisely, it made

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.609
arrangements for its individual

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:45.499
residents to contract with

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.389
that nearby fire department

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:49.159
for fire protection.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:50.359
And there was

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:54.319
an aging man here,

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.299
Gene Cranick, had paid

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:58.399
the fee every year, but

00:04:58.400 --> 00:04:59.449
he's getting old. He starts

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:00.859
forgetting things.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:02.449
He forgets to make the payment one

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:04.369
year and a fire breaks

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:05.959
out at his house and they call the

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:08.209
fire department and they're told

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:10.459
that, sorry, you didn't pay.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:11.359
You can't pay now.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:12.559
It's too late.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:13.909
There's nothing that we can do for

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:15.769
you. And the fire department ends

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:17.569
up coming and they stand there and

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:19.639
they watch his house burn down.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:21.319
They're there in case the house

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:22.579
spreads to the houses of the

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:24.469
neighbors who did pay, because

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:25.669
that's what they understand their

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:27.199
functions be.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.059
And this episode

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:31.309
generates a furious debate

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:32.629
in the press.

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.609
And one of the

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.619
points of agreement between

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:38.689
the left and the right is

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:40.339
that this is the true face of

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.349
libertarianism, that you

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:44.809
have writers on the left saying,

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.669
look, this shows just

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:48.879
how harsh and cruel

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:50.829
unregulated capitalism is

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:52.359
and why we need to get away from

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.639
capitalism and move toward a system

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:56.859
that values people and not profits.

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:58.809
And people on the right saying,

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:00.309
look, the fire department did the

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:02.019
right thing. We've got to get people

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:03.879
to take personal responsibility

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:05.649
for what they are doing.

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:07.599
And what I want to argue is

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.969
that libertarianism has shifted

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:11.229
over time.

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:13.299
It's in its original form

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:15.339
it did not do remarkable

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:16.929
things like help the fire department

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:18.849
stand by and watch somebody's house

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:19.850
burn down.

